---
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/500.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/500.css';
import '@phosphor-icons/web/regular';
import '../styles/wire-terminal.css';

const nav = [
  { href: '#what', label: 'What it does' },
  { href: '#install', label: 'Install' },
  { href: '#formats', label: 'Formats' },
  { href: '#output', label: 'Output' },
];
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>pace-analyzer — per-thread preemption attribution from any trace</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Open-source analyzer that splits every thread's wall time into on-CPU, preempted and blocked, and reports what that means. Reads PACE, Chrome Trace Event, perf sched and perf trace."
    />
    <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'><rect width='32' height='32' fill='%23131311'/><path d='M6 22 L12 10 L18 22 L24 10' stroke='%23e8a33d' stroke-width='2.5' fill='none'/></svg>" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <nav>
      <div class="wrap">
        <a class="brand" href="/PACE/analyzer/">
          <i class="ph ph-waveform" style="font-size:18px"></i>
          pace-analyzer
        </a>
        <div class="links">
          {nav.map((item) => <a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>)}
          <a
            href="https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE"
            class="btn"
            style="padding:5px 12px;font-size:13px"
          >
            <i class="ph ph-github-logo"></i>
            Source
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <div class="wrap">
      <section class="hero" id="what">
        <div class="section-label">Open source, Apache-2.0</div>
        <h1>Which part of the frame was the code, and which part was the scheduler?</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          <strong>pace-analyzer</strong> takes a trace and splits every thread's wall time
          into the three things it can actually be: time on CPU, time lost to OS
          preemption, and time blocked. Then it tells you which threads are worth your
          attention, and why.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          It reads PACE's own traces and traces from other tools, so you can use it to
          evaluate the attribution on a trace you already have.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn primary" href="#install">
            <i class="ph ph-terminal-window"></i>
            Install it
          </a>
          <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE">
            <i class="ph ph-github-logo"></i>
            Read the source
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <div class="section-label">The command</div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="panel terminal-block">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-terminal-window"></i> Terminal report</h3>
            <pre><code>{`$ pace analyze trace.jsonl
PACE thread attribution
======================

samples: 106023 across 48 threads   decomposition: exact

thread           on_cpu    preempted      blocked preempt%
----------------------------------------------------------
3164905       654.75 ms       1.22 s    131.12 ms    60.8%
3164906       673.13 ms       1.18 s    150.41 ms    58.9%
3164924       674.24 ms       1.20 s    130.77 ms    59.8%
...
TOTAL      30824.56 ms      65.31 s       0.00 ms    67.9%

findings
--------
[critical] the run lost 67.9% of its total wall time to preemption`}</code></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-magnifying-glass"></i> What it tells you</h3>
            <p>
              A thread that lost 60&nbsp;% of its wall time to preemption is not a code
              problem. The fix is a different thread count, a different priority, a
              different affinity, or a different machine. A profiler that only reports
              CPU time cannot tell you that, so teams optimise code that was never the
              bottleneck.
            </p>
            <p>
              The analyzer makes that distinction the first thing you see, per thread and
              per phase, and it verifies its own arithmetic on every sample before it
              reports anything.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="install">
        <div class="section-label">Install</div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-cube"></i> From source</h3>
            <pre><code>{`git clone https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE
cd PACE/pace-analyzer
cargo build --release
./target/release/pace --help`}</code></pre>
            <p>
              No runtime dependencies beyond the Rust standard library. The JSON and
              perf text parsing is hand-rolled against the formats' documented shapes
              rather than pulled in as dependencies, so the binary stays small and the
              parsing behaviour stays inspectable.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-terminal"></i> The four commands</h3>
            <pre><code>{`pace analyze <trace>   # the attribution report
pace inspect <trace>  # raw per-thread samples
pace verify <trace>   # check the decomposition invariant
pace convert <in> --to pace|chrome`}</code></pre>
            <p>
              <code>verify</code> exits non-zero when a trace violates
              <code>wall == on_cpu + preempted + blocked</code>, so it can gate a CI job
              that consumes PACE traces.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="formats">
        <div class="section-label">Input formats</div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-file-code"></i> What it reads</h3>
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Format</th>
                  <th>Source</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>PACE JSONL</td>
                  <td>the SDK's own writer</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Chrome Trace Event</td>
                  <td>the standard trace format</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>perf sched timehist</td>
                  <td>perf's scheduling analysis</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>perf trace CSV</td>
                  <td>perf's event trace</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-info"></i> What it does not invent</h3>
            <p>
              A format that carries no attribution is reported as carrying no
              attribution. A plain Chrome trace gives the analyzer a duration and no
              split, so the analyzer prints a dash where the preempted and blocked
              figures would go, draws an empty split bar, and raises no findings
              against that thread. It never prints a confident 0&nbsp;%.
            </p>
            <p>
              Where a platform cannot make a distinction, the report says so instead of
              papering over it. That is the same rule the SDK follows.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="output">
        <div class="section-label">Output</div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-file-html"></i> A self-contained HTML report</h3>
          <p>
            <code>pace analyze trace.jsonl --output report.html</code> writes one file
            that opens offline with nothing to fetch. The per-thread split is drawn
            inline, so there is no charting library to load and no network dependency in
            a report you are about to attach to a bug.
          </p>
          <p>
            The report carries the same information as the terminal output: a summary
            readout, a per-thread table with the split bar, ranked findings, and a
            per-phase breakdown when the trace carries phase labels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="readout" style="margin-top:26px">
          <div class="readout-head">
            <span>pace-analyzer / report</span>
            <span>offline, single file</span>
          </div>
          <div class="readout-body">
            <div class="bar">
              <span class="oncpu" style="width: 32%"></span>
              <span class="preempt" style="width: 54%"></span>
              <span class="blocked" style="width: 14%"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="readout-rows">
              <span class="k">on-CPU</span><span class="v">654.75 ms</span>
              <span class="k">preempted</span><span class="v crit">1.22 s</span>
              <span class="k">blocked</span><span class="v">131.12 ms</span>
              <span class="k">decomposition</span><span class="v">exact</span>
            </div>
            <div class="legend">
              <span><i class="key" style="background: var(--accent)"></i> on-CPU</span>
              <span><i class="key" style="background: var(--sev-critical)"></i> preempted</span>
              <span><i class="key" style="background: var(--sev-info)"></i> blocked</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <div class="section-label">Relationship to the SDK</div>
        <div class="callout">
          <div class="callout-title">Two products, one method</div>
          <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE">pace-sdk</a> is the licensed
            C and C++ library that produces the samples. This analyzer is the
            open-source consumer of them, and the proof that the attribution is real:
            it verifies the decomposition on every sample of a trace and refuses to
            report on a trace that does not hold together.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <footer class="footer wrap">
      <span>pace-analyzer — Apache-2.0</span>
      <span>
        <a href="https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE">Source</a> ·
        <a href="https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE">pace-sdk</a> ·
        <a href="https://perfetto.dev/docs/trace-event-format">Trace format</a>
      </span>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
